        "title":  "When AI explains its decision, humans may stop thinking independently",
        "source":  "CIO.com",
        "url":  "https://www.cio.com/article/4211674/when-ai-explains-its-decision-humans-may-stop-thinking-independently.html",
        "date":  "2026-08-19",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "A Harvard Business School, MIT, and University of Washington study found that when AI systems provide explanations for their decisions, humans become less likely to think independently and more prone to accepting incorrect recommendations. Paradoxically, AI recommendations without explanations led to better decision-making outcomes than those accompanied by narrative rationales.",
        "execSummary":  "Enterprise leaders deploying AI decision-support tools should be cautious about enabling explainability features: new research shows that AI-generated explanations cause humans to defer more — and think less — even when the AI is wrong. A joint study from Harvard, MIT, and UW found that narrative rationales from AI systems correlated with worse human decision quality compared to AI recommendations delivered without explanation. For CIOs and AI strategy teams, this introduces a governance paradox: transparency requirements may inadvertently degrade the human oversight they are meant to support. Design AI workflows to preserve active human judgment, not just visible AI reasoning.",
